body:
Basically all from http://www.ecma-international.org/ecma-262/6.0/#sec-resolveexport
1. Star exports should not include 'default'

   ```
   // entry.js
   import def from './a';
   console.log(def);

   // a.js
   export * from './b';

   // b.js
   export default 'b-default';
   ```

   should log `undefined` because `export * from` only applies to named exports, not `default`. This is the main one on this list that seems like a problem, because it silently does the wrong thing, and users may accidentally rely on it.
2. Local exports take precedence over star exports.

   ```
   // entry.js
   import {foo} from './a';
   console.log(foo);

   // a.js
   export var foo = 'a-foo';
   export * from './b';

   // b.js
   export var foo = 'b-foo';
   ```

   should log `a-foo` since named exports take precedence over star exports. Currently it throws because the `export * from` tries to redefine the non-configurable property.
3. Local exports take precedence over indirect exports

   ```
   // entry.js
   import {foo} from './a';
   console.log(foo);

   // a.js
   export var foo = 'a-foo';
   export {foo} from './b';

   // b.js
   export var foo = 'b-foo';
   ```

   should log `a-foo` since named exports take precedence over indirect exports. Currently it throws because the `export * from` tries to redefine the non-configurable property.
4. Indirect exports take precedence over star exports.

   ```
   // entry.js
   import {foo} from './a';
   console.log(foo);

   // a.js
   export {foo} from './c';
   export * from './b';

   // b.js
   export var foo = 'b-foo';

   // c.js
   export var foo = 'c-foo';
   ```

   should log `c-foo` because indirect exports take precedence.
